{"ex-3B07-1":{"number":"1","title":"Describing a longitudinal wave","question":"<p>The figure below shows the particles on a longitudinal wave travelling to the left at different times <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Find the amplitude, wavelength, period, frequency and speed of the wave.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/94bb4242f96f28b6b8225ac6.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:467.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Particle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is furthest away from its equilibrium position at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 s.</p><p>∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 6 cm</p><p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 3 s, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>b</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>j</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are two adjacent centres of compression.</p><p>∴ λ = 8 × 5 = 40 cm = 0.4 m</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> completes half a vibration from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1 s to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 5 s.</p><p>∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 × (5 − 1) = 8 s</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.125 Hz</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ = (0.125)(0.4) = 0.05 m s<sup>−1</sup></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 4","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":2},"id":"ex-3B07-1","chapter":"3B07"},"ex-3B07-2":{"number":"2","title":"Possible frequencies of a wave","question":"<p>A longitudinal wave travels to the right along a slinky spring. Three snapshots of the spring taken at 0.6 s intervals are as shown (Fig a). Suggest the three minimum possible frequencies of the wave. Explain your answer.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/24888b455d6602908f9eb51e.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:291.7px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.6 s, the wave may have travelled 0.5λ,</p><p>1.5λ (Fig b), 2.5λ, etc.</p><p>If the wave has travelled 0.5λ in 0.6 s,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.833λ</p><p>If the wave has travelled 1.5λ in 0.6 s,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2.5λ</p><p>If the wave has travelled 2.5λ in 0.6 s,</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/d8261e02ef8343a3e6ab9b3b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:295.7px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4.17λ</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.833 Hz</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 2.5 Hz</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 4.17 Hz</p><p>Revision 7 Q6 (p.190)</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 5","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":15},"id":"ex-3B07-2","chapter":"3B07"},"ex-3B07-3":{"number":"3","title":"Motion of particles","question":"<p>The positions of the particles on a longitudinal wave at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are as shown. The wave is travelling to the left.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/fa94914bdf564c16c0c5512e.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:531.0px\"/></p><p>(a) Name a particle vibrating in phase with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Name a particle vibrating in antiphase with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) Name a particle that is momentarily at rest at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The equilibrium positions of g and c are 0.5λ apart, as is the case with the equilibrium positions of h and d.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 6","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":34},"id":"ex-3B07-3","chapter":"3B07"},"ex-3B07-4":{"number":"4","title":"The s–t graph of a longitudinal wave","question":"<p>The figure below shows the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph of particle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> on a longitudinal wave travelling to the right. The displacement towards the right is taken as positive.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2620ca6c1d912461659ed0d2.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:282.6px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the amplitude of the wave.</p><p>(b) Find the frequency of the wave.</p><p>(c) Describe the motion of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at</p><p>(i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0,</p><p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1 s.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) 3 cm</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.25 Hz</p><p>(c) (i) It is moving to the right.</p><p>(ii) It is momentarily at rest.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>s changes from 0 to a positive</p><p>value just after t = 0, so P</p><p>is moving in the positive direction. Pay attention to the positive direction defined in the question. Do not answer upwards.</p>","think":"<p>How will the answers to (c) change if the wave travels towards the left and the displacement towards the left is taken as positive?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>(i) At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, the slope of the given displacement–time graph is positive. With left defined as positive, P is therefore moving to the left. (ii) At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, the graph has zero slope, so P is still momentarily at rest. Particle velocity is determined by the slope of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph and the chosen positive direction, not by the wave’s direction of travel alone.</p>","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 9","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":48},"id":"ex-3B07-4","chapter":"3B07"},"ex-3B07-5":{"number":"5","title":"The s–d graph of a longitudinal wave","question":"<p>A longitudinal wave travels to the right. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph of the wave</p><p>at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 is as shown. The</p><p>displacement to the right is taken as positive. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are particles on the wave.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2e0251bdc40df8c17959b60b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:266.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the wavelength of the wave.</p><p>(b) Name a particle at the centre of compression at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.</p><p>(c) Describe the motion of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) 8 cm</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is moving to the left.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The s–d graph after a very short time:</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a7ae42a250a6d6a263aca66b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:182.5px\"/></p><p>∴ Z is moving in the negative</p><p>direction, i.e. towards left.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 11","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":71},"id":"ex-3B07-5","chapter":"3B07"},"ex-3B07-6":{"number":"6","title":"Measuring the speed of sound in air","question":"<p>Mary does an experiment to measure the speed of sound in air. She connects two microphones <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to a timer (Fig a). She then hits a metal plate with a hammer to make a sound. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> receives the sound, the timer starts. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> receives the sound, the timer stops. The speed of sound can be found from the reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the timer and the distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/80e01fff45ec5c2fc9c17db5.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:542.3px\"/></p><p>(a) At which point in Figure b, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, should Mary make the sound?</p><p>(b) She repeats the experiment with different distances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and records the following data.</p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> / m</p></td><td><p>0.4</p></td><td><p>0.5</p></td><td><p>0.6</p></td><td><p>0.7</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> / ms</p></td><td><p>0.82</p></td><td><p>1.47</p></td><td><p>1.75</p></td><td><p>2.05</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Estimate the speed of sound in air from the data.</p><p>(c) The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> should not be too small. Explain.</p><p>(d) The metal plate should not touch the bench. Explain.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> / m</p></td><td><p>0.4</p></td><td><p>0.5</p></td><td><p>0.6</p></td><td><p>0.7</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> / ms</p></td><td><p>0.82</p></td><td><p>1.47</p></td><td><p>1.75</p></td><td><p>2.05</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> / m s<sup>–1</sup></p></td><td><p>488</p></td><td><p>340</p></td><td><p>343</p></td><td><p>341</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>(b)</p><p>The speed of sound = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 341 m s<sup>–1</sup></p><p>(c) If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is small, the percentage error in measuring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> will be large.</p><p>(d) If the metal plate touches the bench, some sound waves will travel through the bench. Since sound travels much faster in the bench than in air, the experimental results will be affected.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>If she makes the sound at Q, the distance travelled by the sound in time t will be equal to QY – QX, not d.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Taking the average value of several sets of data can average out the error in each set of data. The first data is omitted because it deviates a lot from the other data.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 16","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":90},"id":"ex-3B07-6","chapter":"3B07"},"ex-3B07-7":{"number":"7","title":"Echoes","question":"<p>Steven and Lily, 400 m apart, stand 150 m in front of a smooth vertical cliff. Steven</p><p>shouts once at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0. The speed of</p><p>sound in air is 340 m s<sup>–1</sup>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/967ed884f980f0498a27e258.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:222.1px\"/></p><p>(a) Steven hears the echo at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Lily hears the shout directly from Steven and then its echo from the cliff. Find the time interval between the two sounds.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.882 s</p><p>(b) Steven’s shout travels to Lily along two paths.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1f8ce65b7bcee99a25de7245.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:253.6px\"/></p><p>Length of path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>Difference between the paths <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 500 – 400 = 100 m</p><p>Time interval = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.294 s</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The sound travels from Steven to the cliff and back to him.</p><p>∴ d = 150 × 2 = 300 m</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 18","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":112},"id":"ex-3B07-7","chapter":"3B07"},"ex-3B07-8":{"number":"8","title":"Interference of sound","question":"<p>Two loudspeakers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are connected to a signal generator in a basketball court. The sounds that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> produce have a frequency of 850 Hz and are in phase. Tom walks along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. He stops at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> which is 13.1 m from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and 12.1 m from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The speed of sound in air is 340 m s<sup>–1</sup>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/31e83ee28d437c9cce1c1ba7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:313.5px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the wavelength of the 850-Hz sound.</p><p>(b) Explain whether Tom hears a loud sound or a soft sound at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) State an advantage of carrying out this experiment in an outdoor area rather than inside a laboratory.</p><p>(d) How will the separation between two consecutive loud sounds along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> change if the frequency of the sound increases?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ,</p><p>λ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.4 m</p><p>(b) Path difference at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 13.1 m – 12.1 m = 1.0 m <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> 2.5λ</p><p>∴ Destructive interference occurs and Tom hears a soft sound.</p><p>(c) It reduces the reflection of sound from the surrounding environment.</p><p>(d) If the frequency increases, the wavelength will decrease.</p><p>Therefore, the separation between two consecutive loud sounds along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> will decrease.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The separation between antinodal lines decreases as the wavelength decreases.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 22","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":131},"id":"ex-3B07-8","chapter":"3B07"},"ex-3B07-9":{"number":"9","title":"Musical notes","question":"<p>The waveforms of notes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are as shown, with the same scales in the time and amplitude axes.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/32a8af3d2ecb7a873c96216b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:494.5px\"/></p><p>(a) Compare the loudness of these two notes.</p><p>(b) Compare the pitch of these two notes.</p><p>(c) Do these two notes have the same quality?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is louder than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a higher pitch than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) No, they have different qualities.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>(a) The amplitude of P is larger.</p><p>(b) The period of P is shorter, so its frequency is higher.</p><p>(c) The waveforms are different.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B07  |  Source PDF p. 31","source":{"name":"3B07 Examples.docx","sha256":"e2bbe9cd937bf33eb0534667cf2f23966d5eed7be9d4c1a2580b874f1938f4e1","block":150},"id":"ex-3B07-9","chapter":"3B07"}}